From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 03:03:14 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: retire and recycle one-time command storage on
 completion

Transfer native command buffers and allocators to a completion-owned batch
for one-time Vulkan submissions. Keep separate submitter and callback
references so early feedback cannot release commit input storage.

Retain native queues until completion callbacks finish and guard
asynchronous device reporting with a reference-counted context detached at
Vulkan teardown.

A Metal command allocator owns the storage its encoders record into.
Creating one gives it an empty storage pool, so every encoder in it
allocates from the kernel (IOGPUResourceCreate), and releasing one purges
that whole pool back to the kernel. Releasing a one-time recording's three
allocators in the completion handler would start the next recording empty
again: a stream of submissions would trade command storage with the kernel
twice per submit and never amortize it. A native probe of the loop a
virtualized GL stack drives - draw, glFenceSync, glClientWaitSync, repeat -
spends 60% of the submitting thread inside encoder creation that way,
almost all of it in IOGPUResourceCreate's mach call, with a matching amount
of IOGPUMetalResourcePool purging on Metal's completion thread; one flush
costs 0.56 ms on an M1 Ultra with a single triangle in it.

Hand the allocators of a completed recording to a small device-level cache
instead, resetting each one as Metal requires and releasing anything past
the cache bound, and take from that cache when a command buffer begins. The
cache is refcounted and holds no Vulkan pointers, like the queue's feedback
block, because Metal can dispatch a completion after the device is gone.

kosmickrisp_command_completion covers storage reclamation, rerecording,
delayed feedback, and late error reporting with native weak references.
The command buffers must be gone when the recording retires; the
allocators must still be alive then, must be the very same three the next
recording begins on, and must be gone only once the device and the last
outstanding completion have both let go of the cache. Identity is taken
inside an autorelease pool, because reading a weak reference retains it
until the pool drains and an observer that skips one keeps alive what it
measures.

Device creation now fails with VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY when the device's
allocator cache cannot be allocated. Its unwind frees the device, so every
jump into it has to carry a failure: the two older jumps beside it, for a
Metal compiler and a residency set Metal refuses, now return
VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED and VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY rather than
the VK_SUCCESS of the step before them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
